From 458cc6ce5a1b93ca16f1d7b4c7aefcc6f8ed5019 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aiden Barrett <aidenbarrett101@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 00:13:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Make the checkout deterministic, and stop asserting
 single-config CMake twice

The final two Windows pytest failures. One invalid test assumption, one real
defect in two halves. No runtime behaviour changes, no experimental semantics
change, INSTRUMENT.json untouched, and neither identity moves:

    source_sha256       e16b2f01c4fd4ec4623b4588cffa064fe270ca5ee52b1e290762f8ae91e2566b
    base_prompt_sha256  f1fb88236b18c2aa21d396a91cdc878274005cb47d687b1442811c6a740f1559

1. test_a_cache_with_no_profile_marker_is_treated_as_unknown_provenance

   The same single-config assumption as the two already fixed, in a third
   place. It ended with a literal CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE:STRING=RelWithDebInfo check,
   which asserts that Visual Studio will populate a variable it deliberately
   leaves empty. That is a claim about CMake, not about us.

   The invariant is that an unlabelled cache is treated as unknown provenance
   and reconfigured rather than trusted, and it is now stated in three steps
   that hold under either generator: after deleting PROFILE_STAMP,
   configured_profile() must read None, the cache must still exist, and after
   build_target(profile="release") the generator-aware
   _assert_tree_is_configured_for() must accept it.

   Stronger than what it replaced. The old version never checked that the
   provenance actually read as unknown; it inferred it from a side effect.

2. test_the_declared_identity_matches_this_tree

   Not a hashing defect and not a Windows identity. The tree was
   non-deterministic, in two places, and both have to close or neither works.

   source_sha256 hashes raw file bytes, deliberately. A hash that normalised
   line endings first would be a hash of what we chose to look at rather than
   of what is on disk, and it would forgive any future byte difference somebody
   decided was cosmetic. Keeping it raw means the working tree has to be
   canonical instead.

   a. The checkout. Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true and rewrites LF to
      CRLF for every text file on checkout. Measured on this tree, same source,
      converted:

          LF working tree    e16b2f01c4fd4ec4623b4588cffa064fe270ca5ee52b1e290762f8ae91e2566b
          same tree as CRLF  6ba842bd27204895d9511a04106a81079c82af846429e638d69995f4d64e657c

      Root .gitattributes with `* text=auto eol=lf` overrides core.autocrlf on
      every host. Frozen evidence is additionally pinned binary: text=auto
      already leaves the SQLite dataset alone because git detects the NUL
      bytes, but a dataset should not depend on a heuristic.

   b. The generator, which runs AFTER checkout in every CI job and undoes (a)
      on Windows. benchmark_fixture/cpp_project is inside the hashed surface,
      and main() wrote it through five bare write_text calls. write_text opens
      in text mode, and text mode on Windows translates every "\n" to "\r\n" on
      the way out, so a generator run there rewrote 24 of the 76 hashed files
      as CRLF regardless of what the checkout had produced.

      Every text write now goes through one helper, _write_text, which pins
      newline="\n". That suppresses the translation rather than performing one,
      so nothing else about the generated bytes changes: content, final
      newlines, ordering and file modes are all untouched, and on Linux the
      generated tree is byte-identical before and after this commit.

   Four regressions, two per half. For the checkout: one asserts the result,
   that no file in the hashed set has CRLF in the working tree, so it fails on
   a checkout that ignored the attributes or on a file added later with CRLF in
   the blob; the other asserts the directive itself, because a checkout that
   happened to be clean would pass the first while leaving the next person on
   Windows to rediscover this. For the generator: an AST pass requires every
   write_text/open/write_bytes call in generate_project.py to pin its line
   endings, and asserts no CR exists in the generator source in the first place
   (newline="\n" translates, it does not normalise); plus a direct check that
   the helper puts the bytes of its source string on disk unmodified. The AST
   pass is the one that matters, because the behavioural check is vacuously
   true on Linux and a missing keyword is therefore invisible here.

   Every file in the hashed set is text, 76 of them, no NUL bytes, so a CRLF
   there is always a line ending and never binary content containing 0x0D 0x0A.

WHAT IS VERIFIED HERE AND WHAT IS NOT

   Verified on Linux, Python 3.11.15: the blobs are already LF, so adding the
   attribute leaves every tracked file unmodified. `git ls-files --eol` reports
   i/lf w/lf for 75 of the 76 hashed files and i/none w/none for the one with
   no line endings at all. `git check-attr` confirms source files resolve to
   text=auto eol=lf and results.sqlite3 resolves to binary. The CRLF regression
   was run against a deliberately converted copy of this tree and reported 75
   offenders and a non-zero exit, so it fires rather than merely existing.
   `python benchmark_fixture/generate_project.py` followed by `git diff
   --exit-code -- benchmark_fixture/cpp_project` is clean.

   NOT verified here, and NOT claimed: that a fresh Windows checkout now
   reproduces e16b2f01. It does not. The two halves above are necessary and
   they are not sufficient, and the remaining gap is reproduced exactly rather
   than guessed at. See the follow-up commit; this one deliberately stops at
   the changes that move no identity.

